Pass the environment to the child process on Windows

Two independent halves of the same failure, so they are fixed together.

ConPtyProcess::startProcess() builds a correct UTF-16, double-NUL-terminated
environment block in envArg -- and then hands CreateProcessW a literal NULL,
with the real argument commented out beside it, exactly as it does for the
working directory (see 0006). Nothing the caller supplies reaches the child.
gcc notices the dead variable:

    conptyprocess.cpp:129:12: warning: unused variable 'envArg'

Simply passing envArg is not enough, though, and doing only that would be worse
than the bug. Session::run() hands Windows a bare _environment, which holds
just the few variables Session itself adds -- TERM, COLORFGBG and whatever the
caller appended. The Unix branch a few lines above merges those over the
process's existing environment; the Windows branch never did, because the value
was being discarded anyway. Pass _environment alone and the child launches with
no PATH, no SystemRoot and no TEMP, which most shells do not survive.

So the merge is done first, with QProcessEnvironment -- which is
case-insensitive on Windows, so overriding an inherited variable replaces it
instead of leaving two entries that differ only in case -- and the result is
sorted case-insensitively, which is the order CreateProcess documents for the
environment block.

This is what makes an MSYS2 shell usable rather than merely launchable: MSYSTEM,
MSYS2_PATH_TYPE and CHERE_INVOKING are ordinary environment variables, so with
this in place a caller can select the UCRT64/MINGW64/CLANG64 environment the
normal way, and a shell inherited from an MSYS2 parent already comes up correct.

Verified live: bash.exe under this widget reports MSYSTEM=[UCRT64] and
uname -o = Msys.

Upstream: not submitted; belongs on PR #578, which is not being merged --
         see 0001's header
Applies-to: all environments (the changed code is behind Q_OS_WIN)

--- a/lib/Session.cpp
+++ b/lib/Session.cpp
@@ -30,7 +30,10 @@
 
 // Qt
 #include <QApplication>
+#include <algorithm>
+
 #include <QDir>
+#include <QProcessEnvironment>
 #include <QFile>
 #include <QStringList>
 #include <QSysInfo>
@@ -351,7 +354,25 @@
     const auto size = _emulation->imageSize();
     const int lines = size.height();
     const int cols = size.width();
-    int result = _shellProcess->start(exec, arguments, _initialWorkingDir.isEmpty() ? QDir::currentPath() : _initialWorkingDir, _environment, cols, lines);
+    // _environment holds only the handful of variables this class adds (TERM,
+    // COLORFGBG, ...). The Unix branch above merges them over the process's own
+    // environment; do the same here, or the child starts with nothing -- no
+    // PATH, no SystemRoot -- and most shells will not run at all.
+    // QProcessEnvironment is case-insensitive on Windows, so an override of an
+    // inherited variable replaces it rather than producing a duplicate key.
+    QProcessEnvironment childEnv = QProcessEnvironment::systemEnvironment();
+    for (const QString &variable : std::as_const(_environment)) {
+        const int eq = variable.indexOf(QLatin1Char('='));
+        if (eq > 0)
+            childEnv.insert(variable.left(eq), variable.mid(eq + 1));
+    }
+    // CreateProcess wants the block sorted case-insensitively.
+    QStringList childEnvList = childEnv.toStringList();
+    std::sort(childEnvList.begin(), childEnvList.end(), [](const QString &a, const QString &b) {
+        return a.compare(b, Qt::CaseInsensitive) < 0;
+    });
+
+    int result = _shellProcess->start(exec, arguments, _initialWorkingDir.isEmpty() ? QDir::currentPath() : _initialWorkingDir, childEnvList, cols, lines);
 #endif
 
     if (result < 0) {
